; T2.4 (2026-09 revision): Minimal correction only. Same channel shape as
; the 3 September baseline (simulators/nvmain/Config/DDR5_4800_DRAM.config),
; 64-byte access (tBURST 4, tCCD 4). Cross-check config only; the primary
; DDR5 baseline is DDR5_4800_DRAM_subchannel.config.
;
; ============================================================================
; F4 (2026-09-21): WRITE-PATH AND POWER-DOWN TIMINGS BROUGHT TO JEDEC DDR5-4800
; ============================================================================
; Same correction as DDR5_4800_DRAM_subchannel.config, applied here so the
; cross-check config stays comparable. The only values that differ between the
; two files are tRDPDEN / tWRPDEN / tWRAPDEN, because JEDEC defines them in
; terms of the burst length and this config runs BL8 (tBURST 4) rather than
; BL16 (tBURST 8).
;
; Clock: CLK 2400 -> tCK = 1 / 2400 MHz = 0.4166667 ns. JESD79-5 Table 521
; gives tCK(avg) min 0.416 ns for DDR5-4800, so these agree to 0.7 ps.
;
;   key        old -> new    new in ns   JEDEC source and arithmetic
;   tCWD         7 -> 38      15.833 ns  JESD79-5 Table 471 (standard p.354):
;                                        DDR5-4800B CWL = CL - 2 = 38 nCK.
;   tWTR         5 -> 6        2.500 ns  JESD79-5 Table 521 (standard p.454):
;                                        tWTR_S = max(4 nCK, 2.5 ns) = 6.
;   tWR         10 -> 72      30.000 ns  JESD79-5 Table 521: 29.952 ns in the
;                                        DDR5-4800 column = 72 x 0.416 ns;
;                                        30 / 0.4166667 = 72.0.
;   tRTP         5 -> 18       7.500 ns  JESD79-5 Table 521:
;                                        max(12 nCK, 7.5 ns) = 18.
;   tPD          6 -> 18       7.500 ns  JESD79-5 Table 272 (standard p.144):
;                                        max(7.5 ns, 8 nCK) = 18.
;   tXP          6 -> 18       7.500 ns  JESD79-5 Table 272:
;                                        max(7.5 ns, 8 nCK) = 18.
;   tRDPDEN     24 -> 45      18.750 ns  JESD79-5 Table 272:
;                                        RL + RBL/2 + 1 = 40 + 4 + 1 = 45
;                                        (RL = tAL + tCAS = 0 + 40; BL8 here,
;                                        so RBL/2 = tBURST = 4).
;   tWRPDEN     19 -> 115     47.917 ns  JESD79-5 Table 272:
;                                        WL + WBL/2 + ceil(tWR/tCK) + 1
;                                        = 38 + 4 + 72 + 1 = 115.
;   tWRAPDEN    22 -> 115     47.917 ns  JESD79-5 Table 272:
;                                        WL + WBL/2 + WR + 1 = 38 + 4 + 72 + 1
;                                        = 115, WR being the MR6-programmed
;                                        write recovery in nCK = 72. NVMain
;                                        parses but never reads this key, so
;                                        it is inert.
;
; NOT changed, same reasons as in the subchannel config: tXPDLL (DDR5 has no
; DLL-off slow-exit power-down, JESD79-5 Table 271, and FASTEXIT never reaches
; the PDPS branch), tXS / tXSDLL (self refresh, never entered; only the
; disabled DRAMPower2 trace writer reads them), tOST / tRTRS (not write-path or
; power-down). The activate path was corrected in round 2, below.
;
; ============================================================================
; F4b (2026-09-21, round 2): ACTIVATE PATH ALSO BROUGHT TO JEDEC DDR5-4800
; ============================================================================
; Same correction as DDR5_4800_DRAM_subchannel.config, and for the same reason:
; round 1's own stall counters show rrdWaits accounting for essentially every
; activate stall in all nine runs and fawWaits nonzero in eight of nine, so
; these are binding constraints, not formalities.
;
;   key        old -> new    new in ns   JEDEC source and arithmetic
;   tRRDR        5 -> 8        3.333 ns  JESD79-5 Table 521 (standard p.454):
;                                        tRRD_S = 8 nCK (different bank group);
;                                        tRRD_L = max(8 nCK, 5 ns) = 12 nCK
;                                        (same bank group). NVMain has one
;                                        rank-wide activate-to-activate value
;                                        and no bank groups, so tRRD_S is the
;                                        deliberate DDR5-friendly mapping, as
;                                        for tWTR.
;   tRRDW        5 -> 8        3.333 ns  Same source. DEAD KEY: parsed
;                                        (Params.cpp:342), never read by any
;                                        timing code. Cannot change a result.
;   RAW          4 -> 4        (count)   UNCHANGED, already JEDEC's
;                                        four-activate window.
;   tRAW        20 -> 32      13.333 ns  JESD79-5 Table 521: tFAW_1K =
;                                        Max(32 nCK, 13.312 ns);
;                                        13.312 / 0.4166667 = 31.949 -> 32;
;                                        max(32, 32) = 32. 1 KB page for a x8
;                                        part (Micron [29] Table 1).
;
;   tCCD 4  NOT CHANGED, and this is a KNOWN DIVERGENCE FROM JEDEC, recorded
;           rather than silently fixed. JESD79-5 Table 521 gives tCCD_S = 8 nCK
;           for DDR5-4800 and says the same 8 nCK applies to BL16, BC8(fixed)
;           and BC8(on-the-fly), so a JEDEC DDR5 part cannot issue back-to-back
;           CAS commands 4 cycles apart even with a chopped burst. tCCD 4 here
;           is not a JEDEC claim: it is the defining shape of this cross-check
;           config, the T2.4 decision to reproduce the 3 September baseline's
;           64-bit channel with 64-byte (BL8) access, which is a DDR4-shaped
;           channel that DDR5 does not offer at all. Raising it to 8 would
;           halve this config's peak bandwidth and destroy the very comparison
;           the file exists to make. Consequence to state in any document: this
;           cross-check config's bandwidth and latency advantage over the
;           subchannel baseline is OPTIMISTIC, and it is a legacy-shape
;           reference, not a JEDEC DDR5 configuration. The primary DDR5
;           baseline is DDR5_4800_DRAM_subchannel.config, whose tCCD 8 does
;           equal tCCD_S.
;
; NVMain caveat, same as the subchannel config: NVMain charges tRRDR and
; advances the RAW ring on every REFRESH as well as every ACTIVATE
; (StandardRank.cpp:602-604), which JEDEC does not do for all-bank REFab. Not
; configurable from this file; very slightly pessimistic for DDR5.
;
; PROVENANCE OF THE JEDEC NUMBERS IN THIS FILE: every JESD79-5 value in this
; file was transcribed from the tables of JEDEC Standard No. 79-5 (DDR5 SDRAM,
; July 2020) read from a third-party mirror,
; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RAMGuide/TheRamGuide-WIP-/main/DDR5%20Spec%20JESD79-5.pdf
; retrieved 2026-09-21, because the standard is access-gated at jedec.org and
; no copy exists under documents/. Table and page numbers are given for every
; value so each can be re-verified, and they SHOULD be re-verified against an
; official copy before publication.
; ============================================================================
;
; Memory configuration file examples
; As an example, the Micron DDR3-1333_4Gb_8B_x8 memory chip is defined

;================================================================================
; Interface specifications

; 666 MHz clock (1333 MT/s DDR). Clock period = 1.5 ns
CLK 2400

; Data Rate. 1 for SDR, 2 for DDR
RATE 2

; Bus width in bits. JEDEC standard is 64-bits
BusWidth 64

; Number of bits provided by each device in a rank
; Number of devices is calculated using BusWidth / DeviceWidth.
DeviceWidth 8

; NVMain use CLK 2400
; is actually used. So a simple CLK=1 and CPUFreq=4 still works for simulation. 
; However, it is straightforward to make it informative.
CPUFreq 3000
;================================================================================

;********************************************************************************
; General memory system configuration

; Number of banks per rank 
BANKS 32

; Number of ranks per channel
RANKS 2

; Number of channels in the system
CHANNELS 2

; Number of rows in one bank
ROWS 65536

; Number of VISIBLE columns in one LOGIC bank 
COLS 32

; whether enable sub-array level parallelism (SALP)
; options: 
;   No SALP: MATHeight = ROWS
;   SALP: number of subarrays = ROWS / MATHeight
MATHeight 65536

; Whether use refresh? 
UseRefresh true

; the refresh granularity (the number of banks refreshed together in a rank)
; this must NOT be 0 when UseRefresh is true
; JEDEC recalibration (finding #7, 2026-07-11): DDR5 REFab is all-bank, so set
; to BANKS (32) for a single synchronized all-bank refresh event per rank
; rather than the inherited-from-template 8-of-32 grouping. Verified
; algebraically (and re-verified against the re-run) that this does NOT change
; total refresh energy/power -- SubArray's per-subarray charge
; (EIDD5B-EIDD3N)*tRFC/BANKS times BanksPerRefresh simultaneous subarray calls
; per event, times BANKS/BanksPerRefresh events per tREFI window, is
; (EIDD5B-EIDD3N)*tRFC regardless of BanksPerRefresh. This only changes
; concurrency realism (all banks blocked together during tRFC, matching REFab)
; and the per-bank refresh-event count/size split, not the module-sum total.
BanksPerRefresh 32

; RefreshRows is the number of rows to refresh per refresh operation
; for example, RefreshRows = ROWS/8192 for DRAM
; JEDEC recalibration: ROWS/8192 = 65536/8192 = 8, matching JEDEC's 8192 REF
; commands per tREFW window (was 4, inherited from the DDR3-1333 template).
RefreshRows 8

; the number of refresh that can be delayed
; options: 1 -- 8 (DDR-3 defines the maximum 9*tREFI)
; when 1 is applied, immediate refresh is used, otherwise the refresh can be
; delayed
DelayedRefreshThreshold 1; 
;********************************************************************************

;================================================================================
; Memory device timing parameters (in memory cycle) 

tBURST 4

tCMD 1 
tRAS 77
; Corrected 2026-08: tCAS/tRCD/tRP were 34-34-34 (unsourced/placeholder).
; Real DDR5-4800 standard (non-3DS) speed bin per SK hynix part-number decoder
; (documents/reference_validation_papers/TR-20230620152301091.pdf,
; TR-20230620152301412.pdf, code "EB" = "4800 40-39-39") is 40-39-39.
; [10] (JESD79-5D) itself remains access-gated; this is the best available
; grounding - see docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-29-book-reference-fixes.md.
tRCD 39
tAL 0
; F4b: KNOWN DIVERGENCE FROM JEDEC, deliberately kept. JESD79-5 Table 521
; (p.454) gives tCCD_S = 8 nCK for DDR5-4800, and the same 8 nCK for BL16,
; BC8(fixed) and BC8(on-the-fly); tCCD_L = max(8 nCK, 5 ns) = 12 nCK. tCCD 4
; here is not a JEDEC claim but the defining shape of this cross-check config
; (T2.4: reproduce the 3 September 64-bit channel with 64-byte BL8 access, a
; DDR4-shaped channel DDR5 does not offer). Raising it to 8 would halve this
; config's peak bandwidth and destroy the comparison the file exists for.
; Consequence: this config's speed advantage over the subchannel baseline is
; optimistic. The primary baseline is DDR5_4800_DRAM_subchannel.config, whose
; tCCD 8 does equal tCCD_S.
tCCD 4
; F4: CAS write latency. NVMain charges tCWD directly on the write request's
; own completion event (SubArray.cpp:671 writeEventTime = now + tCWD +
; MAX(tBURST,tCCD)*burstCount + writeTimer), so it IS the write latency, and
; it also sets the read-to-write turnaround (tCAS + tBURST + tRTRS - tCWD).
; With tAL 0 that is JEDEC WL = CWL exactly.
; JESD79-5 Table 471 (p.354): DDR5-4800B CWL = CL - 2 = 38 nCK = 15.833 ns at
; tCK 0.4166667. Was 7 (2.917 ns), the DDR3-1333 template's cycle count.
tCWD 38
; F4: write-to-read, measured from the END of the write burst
; (nextRead = now + tCWD + tBURST + tWTR + writeTimer, SubArray.cpp:656),
; which is the JEDEC definition. NVMain has a single tWTR, models no bank
; groups, and StandardRank.cpp:376 applies it rank-wide, while JEDEC splits it
; into tWTR_L = max(16 nCK, 10 ns) = 24 nCK (same bank group) and
; tWTR_S = max(4 nCK, 2.5 ns) = 6 nCK (different bank group), JESD79-5
; Table 521 (p.454). With 8 bank groups of 4 banks only 1 access in 8 pays
; tWTR_L, so charging tWTR_L rank-wide would make DDR5 slower than any real
; part and tilt the comparison toward this project's own conclusion. tWTR_S is
; the conservative mapping: the common case, and the benefit of the doubt for
; the baseline. 2.5 / 0.4166667 = 6.0, max(4,6) = 6. Was 5 (2.083 ns).
; F4b: this is a deliberate DDR5-FRIENDLY JUDGEMENT, not a transcription, and it
; was reviewed and upheld in round 2. The same rule is applied to tRRDR, and to
; tCCD in the subchannel config. Any document describing this config must say so.
tWTR 6
; F4: write recovery, charged AFTER the burst (nextPrecharge = now + tAL +
; tCWD + tBURST + writeTimer + tWR, SubArray.cpp:644), exactly JEDEC tWR.
; JESD79-5 Table 521 (p.454) DDR5-4800 column: tWR = 29.952 ns, the 30.000 ns
; nominal scaled and rounded down to 1 ps, i.e. exactly 72 x 0.416 ns.
; 30 / 0.4166667 = 72.0 nCK = 30.000 ns. Was 10 (4.167 ns).
tWR 72
tRP 39
tCAS 40

tRTRS 1 ; for DDR-1, tRTRS can be 0
; F4: internal read to precharge (nextPrecharge = now + tAL + tBURST + tRTP
; - tCCD, SubArray.cpp:439), exactly JEDEC tRTP.
; JESD79-5 Table 521 (p.454): tRTP = max(12 nCK, 7.5 ns).
; 7.5 / 0.4166667 = 18.0, max(12, 18) = 18 nCK = 7.500 ns. Was 5 (2.083 ns).
tRTP 18
; JEDEC recalibration (finding #7): tRFC1 @ 16Gb = 295ns * CLK(2400MHz) = 708
; cycles. Old value (107 cycles = 44.58ns @ 2400MHz) reverse-solves to 160.5ns
; at the DDR3-1333 template's original 666.667MHz/1.5ns clock -- inherited,
; never rescaled when CLK was overridden to 2400 for this DDR5 config.
tRFC 708
tOST 1 ; ODT switching time

; F4b: the ONE activate-to-activate constraint NVMain applies, rank-wide, on
; every ACTIVATE (StandardRank.cpp:283) and every REFRESH (:602); rrdWaits
; (:658) measures it and it is the binding activate stall in all nine runs.
; JESD79-5 Table 521 (p.454): tRRD_S = 8 nCK (different bank group);
; tRRD_L = max(8 nCK, 5 ns) = 12 nCK (same bank group). Deliberate
; DDR5-friendly mapping to tRRD_S, same argument as tWTR. Was 5 (2.083 ns).
tRRDR 8
; F4b: DEAD KEY. Parsed at Params.cpp:342, read by no timing code anywhere;
; tRRDR serves both directions. Set to tRRD_S for consistency only.
tRRDW 8

; Row Activation Window (RAW), which specifies the maximum Activation in a
; rolling window. tRAW is the corresponding window width (in cycle).
; Options:
;   for 2D DRAM, RAW = 4, Four Activation Window (FAW)
;   for WideI/O DRAM, RAW = 2, Two Activation Window (TAW)
; F4b: RAW 4 is already JEDEC's four-activate window and is unchanged.
RAW 4
; F4b: JESD79-5 Table 521 (p.454): tFAW_1K = Max(32 nCK, 13.312 ns).
; 13.312 / 0.4166667 = 31.949 -> 32 nCK; max(32, 32) = 32 nCK = 13.333 ns.
; 1 KB page for a x8 part (Micron [29] Table 1). fawWaits is nonzero in eight
; of the nine DDR5 runs, so this constraint does bind. Was 20 (8.333 ns).
tRAW 32

; F4 review caveat (2026-09-21): the tRDPDEN / tWRPDEN / tWRAPDEN formulas below (JESD79-5 Table 272 as
; transcribed via a mirror) were not independently corroborated and may be off by one cycle (0.417 ns).
; They gate power-down ENTRY only, tWRAPDEN is never read by NVMain, and no reported result depends on
; them at that precision. Re-verify against an official copy of the standard before citing the table.
; powerdown mode enter and exit
; F4: all four power-down parameters below come from JESD79-5 Table 272
; ("Power Down Timing Parameters", standard p.144). PowerDownMode is FASTEXIT
; and UseLowPower is true, so these values are live during a run.
;
; Earliest power-down entry after a READ without auto-precharge
; (DDR3Bank.cpp:400-402). JEDEC: tRDPDEN = RL + RBL/2 + 1 nCK.
; RL = tAL + tCAS = 0 + 40; this config runs BL8, tBURST 4 nCK, so RBL/2 = 4.
; 40 + 4 + 1 = 45 nCK = 18.750 ns. Was 24.
tRDPDEN 45
; Earliest power-down entry after a WRITE without auto-precharge
; (DDR3Bank.cpp:487-489). JEDEC: tWRPDEN = WL + WBL/2 + ceil(tWR/tCK) + 1
; (Table 272 note 2 rounds tWR/tCK up) = 38 + 4 + 72 + 1 = 115 nCK
; = 47.917 ns. Was 19.
tWRPDEN 115
; Same after a WRITE with auto-precharge. JEDEC: tWRAPDEN = WL + WBL/2 + WR + 1
; with WR the MR6-programmed write recovery in clock cycles = 72, so
; 38 + 4 + 72 + 1 = 115 nCK = 47.917 ns. Was 22. NVMain parses this key
; (Params.cpp:356) but no timing code reads it, so the value is inert.
tWRAPDEN 115
; Minimum time spent in power-down (StandardRank.cpp:495, DDR3Bank.cpp:229).
; JEDEC Table 272: tPD = max(7.5 ns, 8 nCK). 7.5 / 0.4166667 = 18.0,
; max(18, 8) = 18 nCK = 7.500 ns. Was 6 (2.500 ns).
tPD 18
; Power-down exit to next valid command (StandardRank.cpp:545,549;
; DDR3Bank.cpp:275-290). Reached for both PDA and PDPF, which is what FASTEXIT
; produces. JEDEC Table 272: tXP = max(7.5 ns, 8 nCK) = 18 nCK = 7.500 ns.
; Was 6 (2.500 ns).
tXP 18
; NOT corrected. DDR5 has no DLL-off slow-exit power-down (JESD79-5 Table 271),
; so no JEDEC parameter maps onto tXPDLL, and NVMain only reads it in the
; STANDARDRANK_PDPS branch, which PowerDownMode FASTEXIT never reaches.
tXPDLL 17
; NOT corrected. Self-refresh exit. NVMain never enters self refresh; the only
; reader of tXS/tXSDLL is the DRAMPower2 trace writer, whose AddHook PostTrace
; line is commented out at the bottom of this file. Inert.
tXS 5
tXSDLL 512

; refresh window between two refreshes to a cell (in cycle, e.g., 64ms/tCK)
; options: for DDR3, 64ms (normal) or 32ms (thermal extended)
;          for LPDDR3, 32ms (normal) or 16ms (thermal extended)
;          for 3D DRAM, 32ms/16ms/8ms are all possible
; JEDEC recalibration (finding #7, 2026-07-11): DDR5 tREFW = 32ms * CLK(2400MHz)
; = 76,800,000 cycles. Old value (42,666,667 cycles) reverse-solves to exactly
; 64ms at 666.667MHz/1.5ns -- the DDR3-1333 template's clock, carried over
; unrescaled when CLK was overridden to 2400 for this DDR5 config (see the
; stale "666 MHz clock... Clock period = 1.5ns" comment at the top of this
; file). NVMain's "current" EnergyModel (StandardRank.cpp:977-995) computes
; simulationTime as raw elapsed cycles with no separate CLK-to-ns conversion,
; so cycles here must equal real_seconds * CLK directly -- confirmed no hidden
; internal clock domain applies.
tREFW 76800000
;================================================================================

;********************************************************************************
; Memory device energy and power parameters
 
; Read/write values are in nano Joules
; NOTES:
;   NVSIM energy is per word
;   Erd is the read energy from a single mat
;   Ewr is the write energy (SET or RESET, they are the same)
;   These values are the energys required to read a page into a row buffer.
;
;   Other energy values are taken from CACTI
;

; whether use low power mode?
UseLowPower true

; PowerDown mode 
; Option:
;    FASTEXIT: Precharge PowerDown with Fast Exit
;    SLOWEXIT: Precharge PowerDown with Slow Exit
;    <TO DO>: extend to support more power down mode 
PowerDownMode FASTEXIT

EnergyModel current

; Subarray write energy per bit
Ewrpb 0.000202

Erd 3.405401
Eopenrd 1.081080
Ewr 1.023750
Eref 38.558533
Eactstdby 0.090090
Eprestdby 0.083333
Epda 0.000000
Epdpf 0.078829
Epdps 0.000000

; DRAM style power calculation. All values below in mA, taken from datasheet.

; JEDEC recalibration (finding #8a, 2026-07-11): DDR5 VDD/VDDQ = 1.1V per
; JESD79-5 (was 1.5V, the DDR3/DDR4-era default -- also literally NVMain's
; stock Params.cpp class default, never customized for this config at all).
Voltage 1.1

; Finding #8b (cycle 6c, 2026-07-12): EIDD0-EIDD5B current magnitudes sourced
; from SK hynix 16Gb DDR5 datasheet, x8 @ DDR5-4800 ("hynix floor" -- the
; headline config, persisted here; a Micron-ceiling variant was run in
; parallel as a band, see results/system_v2_3B/ and the cycle-6c report at
; results/cycle6c_ddr5_calibration_and_provenance_report.md). hynix does not
; publish IDD2P/IDD3P: EIDD2P0/EIDD2P1 = Micron IDD2P(88) x hynix:Micron
; IDD2N ratio (49/92) = 46.87; EIDD3P = Micron IDD3P(140) x hynix:Micron
; IDD3N ratio (117.6/142) = 115.94 -- documented interpolation, not measured.
; EIDD1/EIDD2NT/EIDD6 left at stock placeholders: confirmed dead (EIDD1/EIDD6
; parsed but never read by any energy formula; EIDD2NT not even declared in
; Params.h/parsed by Params.cpp -- see cycle-6 EIDD audit), so their value has
; zero effect on any simulated result. EIDD6 (T4-10) remains an unsourced
; 12 mA placeholder here specifically because no SK hynix chip-level IDD6
; datasheet value could be confirmed with confidence (/research pass,
; 2026-09-05, documents/MBMM_Book_Typst/research_notes/item4_ddr5_typical_current.md)
; -- the counterpart Micron-ceiling config (DDR5_4800_DRAM_micron.config) was
; updated to a real, sourced value (Micron [29] Table 6, IDD6N=102mA) since
; that datasheet was findable; no equivalent hynix figure was available to
; interpolate from the way EIDD2P0/EIDD3P above already are.
EIDD0 60.2
EIDD1 54
EIDD2P0 46.87
EIDD2P1 46.87
EIDD2N 49
EIDD2NT 37
EIDD3P 115.94
EIDD3N 117.6
EIDD4R 259
EIDD4W 288
EIDD5B 227
EIDD6 12
;********************************************************************************

;================================================================================
; Memory controller parameters

; Specify which memory controller to use
; options: PerfectMemory, FCFS, FRFCFS, FRFCFS-WQF, DRC (for 3D DRAM Cache)
MEM_CTL FRFCFS

; whether dump the memory request trace?
CTL_DUMP false

; whether use close-page row buffer management policy?
; options: 
;   0--Open-Page, the row will be closed until a row buffer miss occurs
;   1--Relaxed Close-Page, the row will be closed if no other row buffer hit exists
;   2--Restricted Close-Page, the row will be closed immediately, no row
;      buffer hit can be exploited
ClosePage 0

; command scheduling scheme
; options: 0--fixed priority, 1--rank first round-robin, 2--bank first round-robin
ScheduleScheme 2

; address mapping scheme
; options: SA:R:RK:BK:CH:C (SA-Subarray, R-row, C:column, BK:bank, RK:rank, CH:channel)
AddressMappingScheme SA:R:RK:BK:CH:C

; interconnect between controller and memory chips
; options: OffChipBus (for 2D), OnChipBus (for 3D)
INTERCONNECT OffChipBus

; FRFCFS-WQF specific parameters
ReadQueueSize 32 ; read queue size

WriteQueueSize 32 ; write queue size

HighWaterMark 32 ; write drain high watermark. write drain is triggerred if it is reached

LowWaterMark 16 ; write drain low watermark. write drain is stopped if it is reached
;================================================================================

;********************************************************************************
; Simulation control parameters
;
PrintGraphs false
PrintPreTrace false
PreTraceFile mcf.trace
EchoPreTrace false
PeriodicStatsInterval 100000000

TraceReader NVMainTrace
;********************************************************************************

;================================================================================
; Endurance model parameters
; This is used for Non-volatile memory

EnduranceModel NullModel
EnduranceDist Normal
EnduranceDistMean 1000000 
EnduranceDistVariance  100000
FlipNWriteGranularity 32

; Everything below this can be overridden for heterogeneous channels
;CONFIG_CHANNEL0 lp_rram.config
;CONFIG_CHANNEL1 hp_rram.config

; whether set the DRAM in powerdown mode at the beginning?
InitPD false

IgnoreData true
;================================================================================

;AddHook PostTrace
;PostTraceFile drampower2.trace
;PostTraceWriter DRAMPower2Trace
;DRAMPower2XML nvmain_config.xml 

